      '<p><strong>Your result: </strong>{{IMPORTANCE_OF_SEX}}</p><p>Across archetypes, and especially dependent on core motivations, sexuality plays <strong>very different roles</strong> in a person\'s life. For some, sex is a <strong>core pillar of identity, vitality, and self-expression</strong>. For others, it is <strong>one meaningful part among many</strong>, or something that becomes important only under certain conditions, and at certain times.</p><p>The <em>importance of sexuality</em> answers a different question than motivation. It is not <em>why</em> someone has sex, but <strong>how central sexuality is to their sense of aliveness, relationship satisfaction, and personal well-being</strong> at a given point in time.</p><p>These differences explain why partners can deeply care for each other yet experience sexuality as mismatched in priority. Importantly, <strong>no level of importance is better or worse</strong>. Problems usually arise not from "too much" or "too little" sexuality, but from <strong>unspoken differences in how central sex is meant to be</strong>.</p>',
