    title: "Attachment Style: How Safety, Closeness, and Distance Shape Desire",
    generalContent:
      "<p>Attachment style describes how a person relates to <strong>emotional closeness, dependency, reassurance, and separation</strong> in intimate and sexual relationships. It answers questions like:<br /><br />How safe do I feel depending on another person?<br />How do I react to closeness, distance, or emotional uncertainty?</p><p>Attachment style is <strong>not neediness, not independence, and not maturity</strong>. It reflects how the nervous system learned to regulate safety, connection, and threat and how desire organizes itself around those patterns.</p><p>Across archetypes, attachment style strongly shapes how desire emerges, how conflict affects libido, and how sex functions as reassurance, bonding, escape, or regulation.</p><p>Common Attachment Style Patterns Across Archetypes</p><p><strong>Secure attachment<br /></strong>These archetypes generally feel safe with intimacy and autonomy. They can enjoy closeness without losing themselves and tolerate distance without panic. Desire is relatively stable and flexible across relationship phases. <em>(e.g. Sensual Connector, Relational Nurturer, Loyal Ritualist, Spiritual Lover, Curious Apprentice)</em></p><p><strong>Anxious attachment</strong></p><p>These archetypes are highly attuned to signs of closeness or rejection. Desire is often intertwined with reassurance, validation, and fear of loss. Sexuality can become a way to secure connection or soothe anxiety. <em>(e.g. Approval Seeker)</em></p><p><strong>Avoidant attachment</strong></p><p>These archetypes value autonomy and emotional self-containment. They may experience closeness as threatening or overwhelming. Desire often activates through distance, novelty, or control rather than sustained emotional intimacy. <em>(e.g. Spark Seeker, Minimalist Companion, Emotional Voyeur, Analytical Sexualist, Quiet Withdrawer)</em></p><p><strong>Disorganized or mixed attachment<br /></strong>These archetypes experience closeness as both desired and threatening. Desire may surge and collapse unpredictably. Sexuality can oscillate between craving connection and needing escape, intensity, or control. <em>(e.g. Explorer of Edges, Power Orchestrator)</em></p><p><strong>Contextual / adaptive attachment<br /></strong>Some archetypes shift attachment expression depending on partner, power dynamics, or relational safety. Their attachment is less fixed and more situationally activated. <em>(e.g. Spark Seeker, Exhibitionist Performer, Explorer of Edges)</em></p><p>Attachment style is <strong>not static</strong>. It can soften or harden across life stages, relationships, healing, or prolonged stress. Many people experience different attachment expressions in different relational contexts.</p><p>Attachment Style of the {{CORE_ARCHETYPE}}</p>",
